Yes. The work you’d do by hand becomes what the characters do to each other. Two characters can solve the same problem in different ways and have entirely different adventures. Then their answer becomes a person, place, or object in the next problem.

Take the backstage comedy we just made. 2 and 3 are trying to get their act from 2+3i to the venue. Their directions say to multiply by 4-i. They split up because each thinks the other’s route is ridiculous.

The Numbers — “Meet Me at Eleven, Ten”

2 takes the long way. He distributes the multiplication, dealing with each interaction separately. Four makes eight aisle seats and twelve balcony seats. Then -i brings three more seats into the aisle and takes two out of the balcony. By the time 2 gets to 11+10i, he has four receipts and a personal grievance against the elevator.

3 takes a shortcut. He puts 2 and 3 in one room, and 4 and -1 in another. The room totals are 5 and 3. He multiplies them and gets 15, a tally that includes all four possible pairings. Two pairings, 8 and −3, belong to the same-axis crews. He removes those from the tally to isolate the balcony count.

3: Fifteen minus eight is seven. Now take away negative three.
TRAINEE: How do you take away a negative three?
3: Cancel the refund.
TRAINEE: That’s the first thing you’ve said that makes sense.

That leaves 10 on the balcony. The aisle gets 8-(-3)=11, because when two imaginary units meet, i^2=-1 flips the sign. Both crews arrive at 11+10i. Each thinks the other followed them.

The answer starts the next skit

The stage manager takes one look at the two crews and asks, “Which one of you is the original act?”

Now the cast has to solve (4-i)z=11+10i backward. The character they need is 4+i, the plus-one’s mirror twin. When the twins work together, their imaginary parts cancel and they leave behind 17, a real number who has spent the entire episode waiting for a speaking part.

Multiply 11+10i by 4+i and you get 34+51i. Seventeen splits that cast into seventeen identical groups. Each group has two in the aisle and three on the balcony. There’s the original act.

STAGE MANAGER: So which group is the original?
SEVENTEEN IDENTICAL THREES: I am.
2: We’re going to need a bigger cab.

That’s a format you can keep writing. The equation tells you what has to happen. The method tells you how the character gets there. A meticulous character distributes every term. A shortcut artist groups the work and subtracts what they counted along the way. A detective starts at the answer and retraces it. The fun is letting each method succeed in its own way and giving each character something different to complain about when they meet.